# CODEOWNERS
#
# Declares the current owner and instructor as owners of the protected content of this
# laboratory: the automation, the governance configuration and the normative
# documents.
#
# STATUS: this file is currently DECLARATIVE ONLY.
#
# The ruleset `protect-main` ships with `require_code_owner_review: false`,
# because a personal repository with a single active participant has nobody
# else who could satisfy a code owner review. Until the second participant
# accepts the invitation, GitHub would either block every merge or ignore the
# rule, and neither teaches anything.
#
# Ownership is declared here anyway so that turning the rule on later is a
# configuration change and not a redesign.
#
# HOW TO ACTIVATE, after the second participant accepts the collaborator
# invitation (exact steps also in ACESSOS-E-PAPEIS.md):
#
#   1. Require one approval:
#      Settings -> Rules -> protect-main -> Require a pull request before
#      merging -> Required approvals: 1
#      API: rules[type=pull_request].parameters.required_approving_review_count = 1
#
#   2. Require the approval to come from somebody other than the last person
#      who pushed:
#      same screen -> "Require approval of the most recent reviewable push"
#      API: rules[type=pull_request].parameters.require_last_push_approval = true
#
#   3. Require a review from a CODEOWNER, which is what makes this file bind:
#      same screen -> "Require review from Code Owners"
#      API: rules[type=pull_request].parameters.require_code_owner_review = true
#
# Do all three only once the second participant can actually approve, and
# record the change in DECISIONS.md. Note that step 2 and step 3 together mean
# neither listed user can merge their own change to a protected path
# unassisted, which is the intended lesson about separation of duties.

# Everything, unless a more specific rule below overrides it.
# The last matching pattern wins in CODEOWNERS.
